Naht is convention-first: most projects need little or no configuration. When you do need it, it lives
in **`naht.toml`** at the project root, layered over a per-machine config. For how files map to
instances, see [File mapping](#/docs/file-mapping).

## Layered config

Settings are resolved in order, with later layers overriding earlier ones **field by field**:

```
built-in defaults  →  ~/.naht/config.toml  (per-machine)  →  ./naht.toml  (per-project)
```

So you can set a machine-wide default (say, a preferred port) and let individual projects override
only what they need. Unknown keys are rejected (`deny_unknown_fields`): a typo fails loudly instead
of being silently ignored.

## `[project]`

```toml
[project]
name = "my-game"   # defaults to the project directory name when unset
```

| Key | Type | Default | Purpose |
|---|---|---|---|
| `name` | string | directory name | The project's display name. |

## `[serve]`

```toml
[serve]
port = 34872         # localhost port the daemon listens on
place_id = 1234567890 # optional place-id guard (see below)
terrain_sync = false # opt into live terrain blob sync
```

| Key | Type | Default | Purpose |
|---|---|---|---|
| `port` | u16 | `34872` | The localhost port the daemon binds. Overridable per-run with `--port`. |
| `place_id` | u64 | _unset_ | If set, the daemon only connects to the Studio session whose place id matches. `0` is refused: it is the id Studio reports for an unpublished place. |
| `terrain_sync` | bool | `false` | Sync terrain live as an opaque voxel blob. Off by default; when off, terrain is flagged as unsynced. |

<Callout type="important">

**`place_id` is a cheap, high-value safety guard.** With it set, a request whose place id does not match is
refused, so you can't accidentally pour one game's source into another. Without it, the daemon binds the project
to the first place that attaches (recorded in `.naht/state.db`) and refuses any other. Set it on any project
that ships. The place id is not a credential: the project token is (see below).

</Callout>

<Callout type="important">

**The project token is the credential.** `naht serve` creates `.naht/token` on its first run and reuses it after.
The Studio plugin asks for the token once per project and stores it in the plugin settings. Run `naht token` to
print it. Every request except the handshake needs it, so keep the file private: whoever can read it can read and
write the project through the daemon.

</Callout>

## `[limits]`

Caps the daemon enforces. Each key is optional and defaults to the value below. `naht serve` refuses a value of zero, or one above its ceiling, at startup, with an error that names the key. It does so before it takes the instance lock, so a refused configuration leaves nothing locked. Like every key in `naht.toml`, a project value overrides the one in `~/.naht/config.toml`.

```toml
[limits]
terrain_voxels = 1048576        # voxels one live terrain push may carry
inventory_body_bytes = 67108864 # largest POST /inventory body, in bytes
mass_delete_warn = 25           # deletions in one reconcile that trigger a warning
connections = 64                # authenticated connections the daemon serves at once
```

| Key | Type | Default | Ceiling | Purpose |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `terrain_voxels` | integer | `1048576` | `8388608` | Most voxels one live terrain push may carry. The plugin reads this value from the daemon, so it enforces the same cap. A larger push is refused with `413`, and the plugin then asks you to run `naht build`. A blob whose header cannot be read is refused with `400`. |
| `inventory_body_bytes` | integer | `67108864` (64 MiB) | `134217728` (128 MiB) | Largest `POST /inventory` body, in bytes. A larger body gets `413`. Every other route keeps the 64 MiB limit. |
| `mass_delete_warn` | integer | `25` | `100000` | A reconcile that deletes at least this many files logs a warning, so you can check the change was intended. |
| `connections` | integer | `64` | `1024` | Authenticated connections the accept loop serves at once. A connection past the cap waits until a slot frees. Connections that have not passed the token have their own pool of 16 and never take these slots. |

<Callout type="important">

The ceiling of `terrain_voxels` keeps the worst-case terrain blob, a 40-byte header plus 6 bytes per voxel, at about 48 MiB, under the 64 MiB body the other routes accept. Raising `inventory_body_bytes` lets two large `/inventory` bodies sit in memory at once, so raise it only when a project needs it.

</Callout>

## `[assets]`

Open Cloud asset upload. Disabled by default, so the unchanged behavior is to sync asset-id strings as
plain properties. See [Terrain & assets](#/docs/terrain-assets).

```toml
[assets]
enabled = true
api_key_env = "ROBLOX_OPEN_CLOUD_KEY"   # NAME of the env var, never the key itself
```

| Key | Type | Default | Purpose |
|---|---|---|---|
| `enabled` | bool | `false` | Upload local mesh/image files and rewrite their references to `rbxassetid://…`. Uploads happen on `naht build` only; `sync` and `serve` leave references as written. |
| `api_key_env` | string | _unset_ | The **name** of the environment variable holding your Open Cloud key. |

<Callout type="warning">

`api_key_env` holds the *name of an environment variable*, not the key. Naht never reads a literal key
from config, keeping secrets out of files you might commit.

</Callout>

## `[[tree]]`

Explicit instance declarations, for what convention can't express. Each entry is one node:

```toml
[[tree]]
instance = "ReplicatedStorage/Shared"
path = "src/shared"
class = "Folder"

[tree.properties]
Archivable = false
```

| Key | Type | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `instance` | string | The instance's path in the DataModel. |
| `path` | string? | The filesystem path it maps to. Must be relative to the project root and stay inside it: a `..` anywhere in the path, an absolute path or a drive prefix is refused. A segment that names a Windows device (`CON`, `NUL`, `LPT1`, with or without an extension), or that holds a character Windows forbids in file names (`<`, `>`, `"`, `:`, `\`, `?`, `*`, a pipe, or a control byte), is refused too, on every host. `naht serve` refuses the whole config at startup when one of its paths is refused; only `naht build` also checks that the symbolic links on the path stay inside the project. A segment that starts with `.` (`.src`, `packages/.hidden`) is refused too, because the scanner and the watcher ignore dot entries, and `naht serve` then refuses the config at startup. A leading `./`, or a path of only `.` (the project root), is accepted. |
| `class` | string? | The Roblox class name, when not implied by convention. |
| `properties` | table | Non-default properties for this node. |

These are written automatically by `naht init --from-rojo` for the genuine exceptions in a migrated
project: see [Migrating from Rojo](#/docs/migrating).

## Minimal vs. explicit

A brand-new `naht init` writes essentially nothing but a name. Convention handles the rest. Reach for
`naht.toml` only when you need a non-default port, a place-id guard, asset upload, or a tree node
convention can't represent.
